{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.05,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.088,
            0.478,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": "The LCM will be $50 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.108,
            0.714,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "Anshu also tried to remove the bigger common factors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.127,
            0.724,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "“For 630 and 770, I will divide both numbers by 10 first. Now, I can divide them by 7. 10 630, 770 The HCF will be 10 × 7 = 70 The LCM will be $10 \\times 7 \\times 9 \\times 11 = 6930$ 7 63, 77 Can you see why this works? 9, 11"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.74,
            0.076,
            0.889,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.228,
            0.847,
            0.285
          ],
          "content": "We need not restrict ourselves to dividing only one prime factor at a time. Both numbers can be divided by whatever common factor we are able to identify."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.294,
            0.653,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "You can try this method for these pairs of numbers. (a) 90 and 150 (b) 84 and 132"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.807,
            0.3,
            0.922,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.359,
            0.703,
            0.381
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Property Involving both the HCF and the LCM"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.389,
            0.755,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "? Which is greater — the LCM of two numbers or their product?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.42,
            0.847,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "？ You could analyse the above statement using examples. Then try to reason or prove, why the LCM is never greater than the product of the numbers. [**Hint**: Is the product also a common multiple of the two numbers?]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.49,
            0.846,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "There is an interesting relation between the product of two numbers and their HCF and LCM."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.538,
            0.641,
            0.562
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Consider the numbers 105 and 95. Find their LCM."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.564,
            0.521,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": "Factorising them into their primes:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.594,
            0.598,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}105 &= 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\\\95 &= 5 \\times 19 \\\\\\mathrm{LCM} &= 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 19.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.664,
            0.666,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider the product in the factorised form:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.368,
            0.694,
            0.631,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "105 \\times 95 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 19",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.722,
            0.845,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "Is the LCM a factor of the product? If yes, what should it be multiplied with to get the product? It can be seen that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.772,
            0.594,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "105 \\times 95 =  LCM  \\times 5.",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.801,
            0.846,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": "? Explore whether the LCM is a factor of the product in the following cases. If yes, identify the number that the LCM should be multiplied by to get the product. Do you see any pattern? Use these numbers: (a) 45, 105 (b) 275, 352 (c) 222, 370"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.934,
            0.187,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": "62"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
